<p>Israel’s Supreme Court has issued an interim order temporarily suspending a government move to revoke the legal status of 37 foreign aid organisations working in Gaza and the West Bank, allowing them to keep operating while judges review a petition challenging the decision. The ruling gives aid groups a short-term lifeline, easing immediate fears of abrupt shutdowns in a conflict-affected area where many civilians rely on outside assistance. At the heart of the dispute are new Israeli rules that would require aid organisations to provide the names of Palestinian staff. The groups say that could put employees at risk and make their work harder, while the government says the measures are about oversight and regulation. The court’s pause doesn’t settle the legal fight, but it keeps humanitarian operations running as the case moves forward.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>What’s on hold: The interim order prevents the 37 NGOs from immediately losing their legal status, which could have forced them to shut down.</li><li>Rule at issue: The contested regulations would require NGOs to identify Palestinian staff members — a central argument in the petition.</li><li>Aid continuity: For now, the decision lowers the risk of sudden breaks in assistance across Gaza and the West Bank.</li><li>A temporary reprieve: The pause may be time-limited, and the longer-term outcome will depend on the court’s further review.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Aid organisations (petitioners): The NGOs say forcing them to disclose the identities of Palestinian staff could put employees at risk and disrupt aid delivery, which is why they petitioned the Supreme Court. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cd6z382de8yo'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Israeli government: The government has pushed new rules tightening requirements for aid groups, including provisions requiring them to name Palestinian staff, as part of its approach to regulating NGO operations. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/israeli-court-allows-ngos-facing-gaza-ban-keep-operating-now'>Al-Monitor (via Reuters)</a>)</li><li>Israel’s Supreme Court: The court issued an interim order allowing the 37 foreign NGOs to keep working in Gaza and the West Bank while it reviews the challenge to revoking their legal status. (<a href='https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20260227-israel-s-supreme-court-allows-aid-groups-facing-govt-ban-to-keep-working-in-gaza'>France 24</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cd6z382de8yo'>Aid groups in Gaza and West Bank thrown lifeline as Israel court pauses ban threat</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/israeli-court-allows-ngos-facing-gaza-ban-keep-operating-now'>Israeli court allows NGOs facing Gaza ban to keep operating, for now</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20260227-israel-s-supreme-court-allows-aid-groups-facing-govt-ban-to-keep-working-in-gaza'>The Supreme Court of Israel issues an interim order suspending a government ban on 37 foreign non-governmental organizations operating in Gaza and the West Bank, allowing them to continue their operations while the court reviews a petition challenging the revocation of their legal status.</a> - france24.com</li></ul>